paragraph:
Chapter 7 Slope and Association

Previously, you used histograms and box plots to look for Patterns in Data and answer statistical questions. In this chapter, you will continue to look for Patterns in Data while also investigating Inputs and Outputs as you learn how to use scatter plots to determine relationships between two different measures for a set of objects. For example, you could analyze amounts of fertilizer and plant height to answer a question like, "If I give a plant more fertilizer, will it grow taller?"

In Section 7.2, you will use Linear Relationships and Connections Across Representations to examine races called triathlons to discover when rates are the same or different. You will also measure the steepness of lines, a concept called "slope," from situations, tables, and graphs.

Finally, you will revisit Patterns in Data to look at how to make predictions about future events from existing data using trend lines and equations. You will learn about the concept of association to help you describe the relationship between two pieces of data.

In this chapter, you will learn how to:
• create scatter plots that show the relationship between two variables;
• identify associations between sets of data and represent the relationship with a trend line;
• measure the steepness of a line by using slope;
• determine the slope of a line given its equation, its graph, or any two points on the line; and
• write the equation of a trend line to fit linear data.
